{"step_index":10809,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-25T16:17:35Z","thinking":"Observing the UI state, the progress modal should be displayed when a specific process is running on a particular screen. Conversely, if the process is not running, the modal needs to be hidden and the form reset. This logic ensures the UI accurately reflects the process state.\n\n\nThe existing code hints at multi-tab support with a visual process indicator, which should activate the modal. The full-screen modal prevents interaction. Scoping the modal's CSS to the target screen should resolve the blocking overlay issue, allowing the user to interact with other UI elements while the matching process runs.\n\n\nThe modal, absolutely positioned within a relatively positioned screen element, will cover only that specific screen, leaving the sidebar and top bar accessible.  The user can interact with the sidebar and monitor the process in the top bar while the modal is active.  Even with scrollable content within the screen, the absolute modal should only cover the visible portion due to the `position: relative` attribute on the parent screen.\n\n\nPlacing the progress modal within the active screen element seems like the best approach.  Positioning it absolutely within that screen ensures it covers the intended area without interfering with other sections. Resetting scroll position or using a minimum height of 100% on the modal can handle cases where scrolling occurs before the modal appears.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"D:/AI_Engineer/Pharmacy-agy-vision/desktop_app/templates/index.html","EndLine":1585,"StartLine":1500,"toolAction":"Viewing screen-new HTML structure","toolSummary":"View screen-new HTML"}}]}
